## Unnecessary Re-renders in React

### 1. Reference Equality

React uses `Object.is` to compare props. Primitives compare by value; objects, arrays, and functions compare by **reference** — a new reference every render means a new prop, even if the content is identical.

```jsx
// Every render creates a new object reference → child always re-renders
<Child style={{ color: 'red' }} />

// Every render creates a new function reference → same problem
<Child onClick={() => doSomething()} />
```

### 2. React.memo, useMemo, useCallback

| Tool | Memoizes | Use when |
|---|---|---|
| `React.memo` | A **component** — skips re-render if props are shallowly equal | Child is pure and receives stable props |
| `useMemo` | A **computed value** — recalculates only when deps change | Expensive calculation; object/array passed as prop |
| `useCallback` | A **function reference** — stable across renders | Function passed as prop to a memoized child |

**Key rule:** `useCallback`/`useMemo` only pay off when the consumer actually does a reference check (i.e., it's wrapped in `React.memo` or used as a hook dependency). Memoizing everything blindly adds overhead with no benefit.

### 3. Context Provider Pitfalls

The entire subtree consuming a context re-renders when the context **value reference** changes — which happens every render if you inline the value:

```jsx
// BAD — new object every render, all consumers re-render
function Provider({ children }) {
  const [user, setUser] = useState(null);
  return (
    <UserContext.Provider value={{ user, setUser }}>
      {children}
    </UserContext.Provider>
  );
}

// GOOD — stable reference
function Provider({ children }) {
  const [user, setUser] = useState(null);
  const value = useMemo(() => ({ user, setUser }), [user]);
  return (
    <UserContext.Provider value={value}>
      {children}
    </UserContext.Provider>
  );
}
```

Another pattern: **split contexts** — separate frequently-changing state from stable dispatch/setters so consumers only subscribe to what they need.

### 4. Before / After Example

**Before** — `ExpensiveList` re-renders on every keystroke because `filters` and `onSelect` are new references each render:

```jsx
function Dashboard() {
  const [query, setQuery] = useState('');
  const [selected, setSelected] = useState(null);
  const items = useData(); // some data source

  return (
    <>
      <input value={query} onChange={e => setQuery(e.target.value)} />
      <ExpensiveList
        filters={{ query }}           // new object every render
        items={items}
        onSelect={(id) => setSelected(id)}  // new function every render
      />
    </>
  );
}
```

**After** — stable references mean `ExpensiveList` only re-renders when `items`, `query`, or `setSelected` actually change:

```jsx
const ExpensiveList = React.memo(function ExpensiveList({ filters, items, onSelect }) {
  // ...
});

function Dashboard() {
  const [query, setQuery] = useState('');
  const [selected, setSelected] = useState(null);
  const items = useData();

  const filters = useMemo(() => ({ query }), [query]);
  const handleSelect = useCallback((id) => setSelected(id), []);

  return (
    <>
      <input value={query} onChange={e => setQuery(e.target.value)} />
      <ExpensiveList filters={filters} items={items} onSelect={handleSelect} />
    </>
  );
}
```

**The fix chain:** `React.memo` on the child makes reference checks matter → `useMemo`/`useCallback` in the parent keeps those references stable.

### Quick mental checklist

1. Is the child wrapped in `React.memo`? If not, memoizing props has no effect.
2. Are you passing inline objects, arrays, or functions? Extract with `useMemo`/`useCallback`.
3. Does your context value change shape on every render? Memoize it.
4. Reach for the React DevTools **Profiler** to confirm a re-render problem exists before optimizing — premature memoization adds complexity for no gain.
